distribution. Lastly, we note that group fairness guarantees are susceptible to compromise under
adversarial conditions. This occurs primarily when the sampling process fails to accurately estimate
the cardinality of element types, as seen in cases of extreme distributional drift. Section 8.3 details
a mitigation strategy for these instances, which requires a larger memory footprint.
4 Computing The Column Widths
Our algorithm is straightforward; we should compute the integer value of 𝑤g1such that Equation (5)
holds. We note that Equation (5)may not hold for an integer value of 𝑤g1. In this case the goal is to
compute the integer value of 𝑤g1such that the left side of Equation (5)is as close as possible to the
right side of Equation (5). A naive implementation is to try every value of 𝑤g1and calculate the
value of the left and right side of Equation (5)by simply evaluate every term in the sum. Such an
implementation would take Ω(𝑛3)time because we should try 𝑂(𝑛) values of𝑤g1, and for each
such value the calculation of each side of Equation (5)takesΩ(𝑛2)time to evaluate the summations.
We first show an exact near-linear time algorithm to compute the value of 𝑤g1that solves
Equation 5 and then we briefly discuss an approximation algorithm that is used in our experiments
to efficiently evaluate the sums of the binomial function.
Efficient exact computation.We first assume that we have two groups, the unpopular elements
g1and the popular elements g2. Then we extend our method to multiple groups G={ g1,..., gℓ}.
Similarly, to the previous section, let E(𝑛 g1,𝑑,𝑤 g1)be the function that represents the left side
of Equation 5. Equivalently, the right side of Equation (5)is captured byE(𝑛 g2,𝑑,𝑤−𝑤 g1). By
definition, the function Ecomputes the expected error of group g1(resp. g2) elements. The values
𝑛g1,𝑛g2, and𝑑are fixed, so the more bins allocated to group g1(respectively, g2) elements, the
smaller the expected error. Hence, the function E(𝑛 g1,𝑑,𝑤 g1)is monotone decreasing with respect
to𝑤g1, whileE(𝑛 g2,𝑑,𝑤−𝑤 g1)is monotone increasing with respect to 𝑤g1. We formally establish
the monotonicity ofEin the following proposition. The proof is shown in Appendix B.
Proposition 1.For fixed𝑛and𝑑, the functionE(𝑛,𝑑,𝑤)is decreasing with respect to𝑤.
This property leads to the observation that instead of trying all values of 𝑤g1, we can run a
binary search in the range [1,...,𝑛] and compute the best value of 𝑤g1. The overall idea of our
algorithm is the following. For every value of 𝑤g1in the binary search, we evaluate E(𝑛 g1,𝑑,𝑤 g1)
andE(𝑛 g2,𝑑,𝑤−𝑤 g1). IfE(𝑛 g1,𝑑,𝑤 g1)<E(𝑛 g2,𝑑,𝑤−𝑤 g1)then we try larger values of 𝑤g1in
the binary search. Otherwise, we continue the binary search with smaller values. In the end, we
return the value of 𝑤g1found by the binary search such that |E(𝑛 g1,𝑑,𝑤 g1)−E(𝑛 g2,𝑑,𝑤−𝑤 g1)|is
minimized.
Even if someone naively applies binary search, the running time would be Ω(𝑛2)to evaluate
